WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.702
Right now, Republicans are inching closer

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:03.269
to winning

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.037
what will be a narrow

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:04.971
majority in the House,

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:06.272
but a majority nonetheless.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:07.874
Let's bring in CNN anchor John Berman.

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:09.275
He's at the magic wall John.

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.111
You know, a lot of these close races

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:12.712
have been tipping red

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:14.547
in the last couple of days.

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.449
So where do we see this stand?

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:18.418
And are we going to see a power shift

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:19.552
as soon as today?

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:21.421
The Republicans have it within sight

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:22.655
at this point, Jim.

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:24.324
They control 215 seats.

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:26.659
We've called 215 races for them.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.395
204 for the Democrats.

00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:29.963
The Republicans only need

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:31.631
three more seats to control

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:32.632
the House of Representatives.

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:34.167
That will give them to 18.

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.236
The Democrats would need to win 14.

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:37.570
And at this point

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.707
there are only 16 uncalled races.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:42.509
You can see Republicans lead in six.

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.344
They only need three Democrats

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:46.579
lead and ten, but they need to win 14.

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:49.249
It is pretty easy to see on this map

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:50.550
where Republicans could pick up

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.951
those three seats that they need.

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:52.986
I'll just write three up here

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:54.020
to remind us

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.189
of what we're looking at in California,

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:57.524
where a lot of these seats are.

00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:57.991
By the way,

00:00:57.991 --> 00:00:59.759
you think the blue state of California

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:00.460
the Republicans

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.027
could, in fact,

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:02.262
crossed that threshold

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:04.364
with just California races?

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:05.265
You have California's

00:01:05.265 --> 00:01:06.566
third congressional district.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.068
This is actually a Republican

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:09.602
leaning district.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:10.804
R plus one.

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:11.471
And the Republican

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:13.440
there is ahead by 9000 votes.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.808
If you go down to the southern part

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:15.708
of the state,

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:17.010
California's 41st

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:17.811
congressional district,

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:19.412
longtime incumbent Ken Calvert

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.880
he served for decades in the House

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:21.648
of Representatives.

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.216
He holds a 5000 vote

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:25.618
lead with 64% at this point.

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.554
This two is a Republican

00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:28.288
leaning district.

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:29.856
So just win those two Republican

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:30.790
leaning districts

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.792
and they're right on the cusp, Jim.

00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:34.527
And then there are also seats alone.

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:35.562
And right now

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:38.331
you have, for instance, California's 45th

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:39.165
congressional district

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:40.633
you have incumbent Michelle Steele,

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:41.701
who is leading ten.

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:42.602
This is a Democratic

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:43.269
leaning district four.

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:45.572
The turnout tends to favor Republicans.

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:47.907
She's ahead by 13,000 votes.

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:49.442
These three races

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:51.511
would give the Republicans control.

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:52.378
And then you pull out

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:53.480
and we go to Colorado.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.781
This race has received

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:55.648
a lot of attention.

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:56.616
This is Lauren Boebert,

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:57.884
California's third.

00:01:57.884 --> 00:02:00.453
She's ahead by 1100 votes. 99%.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:01.921
And all that's left here

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:02.889
is the overseas

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:05.058
military and absentee ballots.

00:02:05.058 --> 00:02:06.693
Could that be enough for the Democrats

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:07.227
to take the lead?

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:07.961
Maybe.

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:09.462
But most Democrats and Republicans

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.131
you talk to around the country doubt it.

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:12.031
So right there,

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:13.500
that would give Republicans

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.534
four of the seats

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:16.169
that they need and control

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.770
of the House of Representatives, Jim.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:18.838
And, you know, it's interesting.

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:21.941
I mentioned Blue California and the fact

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:23.309
that California could be the state

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.011
to put Republicans over the top.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:26.779
If you look two years ago,

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:28.481
these districts are drawn

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:29.849
45 of them were Biden

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.784
districts, seven were Trump districts.

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:35.021
Now, you could have 41 in Democratic

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:36.890
hands. 11 in Republican hands.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:39.692
That's a net minus of four seats there.

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:40.827
That might be the difference

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:42.162
in control for the Democrats, Jim.

